WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.129
And it seemed to me th- the stupidest

00:00:02.129 --> 00:00:04.470
problem you could conceivably imagine

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:07.170
that led to the most complicated answer

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.410
you could conceivably imagine.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:14.040
I was in Cambridge; I finished
a lecture course

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.440
two lectures early, and then I sort of

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.529
hinted, you know, I said the University

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:21.689
regulations or something demand that I

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:24.269
turn up on Friday but you don't have to

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:25.980
turn up on Friday. And I thought they'd

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.410
taken the hint, because when I get to the

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.900
classroom there was usually-
you know, a classroom

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.000
with 150 people in it you can usually

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.510
hear some chatter and so on, but it was

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.940
all quiet. And then I opened the door and

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.710
they said 'surprise!' And 
they'd equipped it

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.590
all for a party. Some of the girls wore

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.489
nice long party dresses, none of the boys

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:52.680
wore long party dresses or if they did

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.239
they were indistinguishable from girls.

00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:57.840
And- but anyway, one of them came up

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.010
to me with this problem,

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:07.140
he said 1, 1 1, 2 1, 1 2 1 1;
and I gathered

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.760
that I was supposed to guess what came

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.080
next. And I didn't succeed;

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:17.615
111221. And he looked at me, and I still
didn't guess.

00:01:18.435 --> 00:01:23.280
312211. And I knew

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.810
from the way he was saying it that

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.090
somehow I was supposed to be able to

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:33.979
guess, I still didn't; 13112221.

00:01:33.979 --> 00:01:36.869
Anyway, it was quite interesting at this

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:40.560
party but I didn't guess - and I suppose I

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.770
should cut the story short a little bit -

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:47.070
in the end he had to tell me. So there's

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.280
no need to be embarrassed if you didn't

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.340
get the rule. What's this sequence
consist of? 1- no,

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.579
how many 1s? One 1, oh yes. What's this

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:00.659
next sequence consist of? Oh two 1s,

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:03.509
oh yes that's right. This next
one is one 2

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:08.459
followed by one 1, oh yes. One 1,
one 2, two 1 - you

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:09.599
get the rule.

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:13.439
And that's quite an interesting sequence.

00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:16.859
And I was just going off at the end of

00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:20.760
term and on the plane to, Boston I think

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:25.639
I was going to, I fiddled with this and

00:02:25.639 --> 00:02:29.069
came up with a really rather remarkable

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:33.540
conclusion. On average each of these

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:39.150
sequences is about 1. - I hope

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.967
I'm getting it right - 1.357

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:46.560
times the one before it.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.579
I mean, not exactly, but it gets closer to

00:02:50.579 --> 00:02:55.709
being this fixed number times the length

00:02:55.709 --> 00:02:57.659
of the one in front of it. So it gets

00:02:57.659 --> 00:03:00.959
bigger and bigger and bigger by powers

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:02.909
of 1.357 all the

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:08.489
time. And I proved that that number - I

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:11.790
hope I've got it right by the way - was an

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.590
algebraic number, don't worry if you

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.150
don't know what that means, of degree 71.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.370
And it seemed to me this- the stupidest

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.709
problem you could conceivably imagine

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:25.409
that led to the most complicated answer

00:03:25.409 --> 00:03:28.889
you could conceivably imagine. Anyway,

00:03:28.889 --> 00:03:30.539
that's been called by some people a

00:03:30.539 --> 00:03:35.099
look-and-say sequence.
I callled it- I wrote a

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:36.509
little paper about it in the

00:03:36.509 --> 00:03:39.290
undergraduate magazine of Cambridge. And

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.389
I called it the weird and wonderful

00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:46.019
chemistry of audio active decay. Audio

00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:49.470
active because you say this, and then in

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.319
some sense the structure decays although

00:03:52.319 --> 00:03:54.150
the thing's getting longer all the time.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.359
And it's like radioactive. And I showed

00:03:57.359 --> 00:04:00.870
that there are some sequences; in fact if

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.689
I go one step further, or maybe two steps

00:04:03.689 --> 00:04:10.949
further, 1113213211, yes

00:04:10.949 --> 00:04:14.590
this is it. If I take a red

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.209
pen so that you can see it more easily;

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:19.660
I'm putting a red spot there. It turns

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.690
out that if you develop this sequence

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:29.290
here it always ends in a 2. Because, you

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:31.090
know, you've got one two at the end of

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.740
it next time. Here you've got maybe

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.110
it's two 2s, I don't know. But anyway

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.680
it'll be 2s. So the the left-hand

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.720
sequence always ends in a 2. And the

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.449
right end sequence, well look: it goes 1

00:04:46.449 --> 00:04:49.810
1 1 3 1 2 - something like that -

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:58.170
and then 3 1 1 3 1 1; and then 1 3 2 1 1 3

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:02.919
etcetera; and then 1 1 1 3 1 2. And here

00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:06.430
we are - this sequence and 
this sequence

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.820
begin in the same way. And you can see

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:14.710
that- so this sequence will begin 3 1 1 3

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:17.199
and so on and this is 1 3 2; what

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:19.150
I'm trying to say is that this will

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.760
always begin with a 1 or a 3. And I

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.770
remarked before that
this always ends in the

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.280
2. So what's interesting about that is,

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.820
if you follow this sequence as if it's

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:35.830
all there is,
and you follow this sequence as

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.960
if it's all there is, 
and then you stick the

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.080
results together it's correct. And so I

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:44.650
say this sequence splits, it's a compound

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:49.810
of two atoms or elements. And it turns

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:53.650
out that there are precisely 92 common

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.870
elements in this thing. And you
know there are

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.590
92- historically there were 92 common

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.130
elements in the world. And that just took

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:04.720
my fancy, there's no connection

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.650
whatsoever, and but I call these elements:

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.900
hydrogen, helium, lithium,
beryllium, boron,

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:11.900
carbon,

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.410
nitrogen, oxygen and so on. 
- (Brady: All the way)

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:18.860
(through to uranium?) 
- Yes. This sequence of

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:23.960
length 10 is not an element, it's

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:26.990
the compound of this sequence 1 1 1 3 2

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:30.410
and the sequence 1 3 2 1 1 in that if

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.050
you follow the- let's call those
L and R. If

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:34.960
you follow L and follow R

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.420
independently you still get the right
answer.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.030
You only need to consider elements that

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:44.690
aren't compounds or sequences that aren't

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:46.820
compounds and we call those elements or

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:50.360
atoms. And it turns out there are 92 of

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:52.280
them. I mean you can do various other

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:56.720
things; for instance I gave a lecture on

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.120
this in Acadia University in Nova Scotia.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.010
There was this Nobel Prize winning

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:07.190
physicist, David Park, at the lecture and

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.260
he said, what- have you thought about

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:16.160
Roman numerals? One, one one. Oh this is

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:28.760
two ones, this is three ones, oh this is

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:32.180
four ones - now the Roman numeral for four

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:44.030
is IV. And then this is 1 1 1 5 1 1. This

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.340
guy gives rise to another problem of the

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.310
same kind and it turns out that this

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:52.190
depends on the root of a twentieth degree
equation.

